      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Both pages are genus-level, not A. africanus alone; RHS: deciduous types are the hardiest, evergreens often tender and best overwintered in a cool greenhouse (A. praecox is the evergreen one that shops in Spain sell; it is not stored as a synonym). Watering: RHS says little water once established, container plants should not dry out completely from summer to early autumn; NC State gives no frequency; let_top_dry is the agent's mapping, interval 5 days a guess. Conflict: RHS says pot-bound plants flower poorly and to pot up 2.5 to 5 cm every two or three years; NC State says let it become rootbound because it blooms best that way; the RHS reading is the one the tip follows. Light: RHS full sun, shade reduces flowering; NC State full sun to partial shade. Size left unknown: RHS 20 to 60 cm up to 1.5 m by cultivar, no spread. Growth medium and habit erect or arching are the NC State genus reading. Hardiness: RHS gives no rating, wrap pots in fleece from November to early April; NC State USDA zones 8a to 11b. Toxicity UNKNOWN for pets: no pet source was found (no ASPCA page for agapanthus turned up in search; NC State has no pet tags). NC State rates the sap low severity for humans (may irritate skin and eyes, mouth ulceration) but its own 'causes contact dermatitis' field says no; because the status is unknown no human exposure is stored. On the pets-unknown list. Es and it names are machine drafts."
      },
